use std
into crowd.amni

The `crowd` library: many agents walking to goals over a `nav` mesh without bumping into each other, by optimal reciprocal collision avoidance (ORCA: van den Berg, Guy, Lin and Manocha, "Reciprocal n-body collision avoidance", 2011); namespace crowd, imports math, vec and nav.mesh. Already provided and never defined here: `nav::path(a, b, out) bool`, which fills a growable float3 array (by reference) with corners from a to b on the mesh and is false when either end is off the mesh or the mesh isn't built; and `nav::locate(p) int, float3`, the nearest walkable cell (-1 when there is none or the mesh isn't built) and the nearest point on it, with the surface height. Numbers are f32. Avoidance is planar: its 2D vectors are float2 where x is world x and y is world z.

Agents live in module growable arrays, one entry each: `POS` and `VEL` (float3), `NEXT` (float2, the velocity chosen this step), `RAD` and `SPEED` (f32), `GOAL` (float3), `MOVING` (bool), `CORNERS` (a growable array per agent of float3 corners) and `AT` (int, the corner being walked to). Settings are module variables: `neighbor_dist` 3, `max_neighbors` 10, `time_horizon` 2, `avoid` true, `arrive` 0.1, `on_mesh` true (stay on the nav mesh when one is built), `keep_right` 0.07 and `nudge` 0.02.

`clear()` empties every agent array. `count() int`. `add(p, radius, speed) int` appends an agent at p at rest, not moving, with no corners, and returns its index. `pos(i)` and `vel(i)` read them; `arrived(i) bool` is true when not moving.

`set_goal(i, g)`: GOAL becomes g, MOVING true and AT 0; the corners are `nav::path` from the agent's position to g when `on_mesh` and it succeeds (dropping a first corner within 0.01 of the position horizontally, but never the last), otherwise just g.

`prefer(i, dt) float2` is the velocity the agent wants: zero when not moving. While AT is not the last corner and the horizontal distance to that corner is under the agent's radius, AT moves on. With d the horizontal vector to the corner and l its length: at the last corner, when l is under `arrive` MOVING becomes false and the result is zero; there the speed is min(SPEED, l / dt) so it never overshoots; elsewhere SPEED; the result is d / l times that speed.

`det(a, b) f32` is a.x × b.y - a.y × b.x. A `Line` holds point and direction (float2).

`lp1(lines, k, radius, opt, dir_opt, result) bool`, result by reference, ORCA's one-dimensional program on line k: with p and d its point and direction, dp = dot(p, d) and disc = dp² + radius² - dot(p, p); false when disc is below 0; t_left = -dp - √disc and t_right = -dp + √disc. For every earlier line i (index below k) with point p_i and direction d_i: den = det(d, d_i) and num = det(d_i, p - p_i); when |den| is at most 1e-5, return false if num is below 0, else skip that line; otherwise t = num / den, and when den is at least 0 t_right = min(t_right, t), else t_left = max(t_left, t); false when t_left > t_right. Then, when dir_opt, result is p + d × t_right if dot(opt, d) > 0, else p + d × t_left; otherwise t = dot(d, opt - p) and result is p + d × clamp(t, t_left, t_right). True.

`lp2(lines, radius, opt, dir_opt, result) int`, result by reference: result starts as opt × radius when dir_opt, else opt shortened to length radius when it is longer, else opt. For each line i in order, when det(d_i, p_i - result) > 0 (result is on the wrong side): copy result, and when `lp1(lines, i, radius, opt, dir_opt, result)` is false restore the copy and return i. Returns the number of lines.

`lp3(lines, begin, radius, result)`, result by reference, used when the constraints conflict: distance = 0; for each line i from begin: when det(d_i, p_i - result) > distance, build a new list: for every j before i, dd = det(d_i, d_j); when |dd| is at most 1e-5, skip j when dot(d_i, d_j) > 0, else its point is (p_i + p_j) / 2; otherwise its point is p_i + d_i × (det(d_j, p_i - p_j) / dd); its direction is (d_j - d_i) normalized. Copy result; when `lp2(new list, radius, (-d_i.y, d_i.x), true, result)` is below the new list's length, restore the copy. Then distance = det(d_i, p_i - result).

`GRID`, a dictionary from int to a growable int array, buckets agents each step by cell (floor(x / neighbor_dist), floor(z / neighbor_dist)) with key cx × 73856093 xor cz × 19349663. `bucket()` rebuilds it. `neighbors(i)` returns a growable int array of the other agents in the 3 by 3 cells around agent i whose horizontal distance is under neighbor_dist, nearest first, at most max_neighbors.

`velocity(i, dt) float2`: pref = `prefer(i, dt)`; without `avoid` that is the answer. Otherwise pref is first turned a little to keep right, which breaks the perfect symmetry that would freeze a ring of agents: with h the fractional part of i × 0.618034 and angle a = -(keep_right + nudge × (h - 0.5)), pref becomes (pref.x × cos a - pref.y × sin a, pref.x × sin a + pref.y × cos a). Then one ORCA line per neighbor j, with inv_th = 1 / time_horizon, rel_pos = pos_j - pos_i and rel_vel = vel_i - vel_j (horizontal, as float2), dist_sq = dot(rel_pos, rel_pos), comb = r_i + r_j. When dist_sq > comb²: w = rel_vel - rel_pos × inv_th, wl_sq = dot(w, w), d1 = dot(w, rel_pos); when d1 < 0 and d1² > comb² × wl_sq, with wl = √wl_sq and unit = w / wl: direction (unit.y, -unit.x) and u = unit × (comb × inv_th - wl); otherwise leg = √(dist_sq - comb²), and when det(rel_pos, w) > 0 the direction is (rel_pos.x × leg - rel_pos.y × comb, rel_pos.x × comb + rel_pos.y × leg) / dist_sq, else it is minus (rel_pos.x × leg + rel_pos.y × comb, -rel_pos.x × comb + rel_pos.y × leg) / dist_sq; u = direction × dot(rel_vel, direction) - rel_vel. When already overlapping: inv_dt = 1 / dt, w = rel_vel - rel_pos × inv_dt, wl its length (use unit (1, 0) when wl is under 1e-9), unit = w / wl, direction (unit.y, -unit.x) and u = unit × (comb × inv_dt - wl). The line's point is vel_i (horizontal) + u × 0.5. Then fail = `lp2(lines, SPEED[i], pref, false, result)` and when fail is below the line count `lp3(lines, fail, SPEED[i], result)`; the answer is result.

`step(dt)`: `bucket()`, then NEXT[i] = `velocity(i, dt)` for every agent (every agent reads positions and velocities from before this step), then for every agent VEL[i] = (NEXT.x, 0, NEXT.y) and the new position is POS + VEL × dt; when `on_mesh` and `nav::locate` of it finds a cell (0 or more), the position becomes that point (on the mesh, at the surface height). POS takes the new position.
